at the outbound  distance away from the food is shorter  on average than the distance between the  foods which means that their algorithm  somehow is able to keep track of what  direction they're walking in and sure  that they cover both of the food sites  so in this one food case again we termed  the r0 the distance between the food in  the first turn now we turn term r0 the  distance between the first food that  they encounter in that first turn so  this is effectively saying that this is  still a path integration problem but now  we're going to be integrating from the  first food that we found so that's when  our step counter is enabled and when we  plot this data we not only find the same  linear relationship but we find the same  C value as well which told us that even  though there was a second food found the  Flies for an undergoing any sort of  state change they were using the same  algorithm to handle both the search in  the month  case in the tooth loop case using our  integrated relationship we now have this  parsimonious model of how lies our path  integrating to perform localization so  they go outbound from their first food  at some point they execute a turn and  ask this reversal they choose their next  run length based on the integrated  distance from the food to that turn plus  some constant C on that way on that  trajectory they come across another food  and that resets their counter at the  reversal they choose their next run  length and so on but at some point  because of the variability in their  initial run length they can find the  second food and when this happens at  that next reversal because their  integrator started at that first good  site they're ensured that their next run  will pass both of those foods this  happens in both directions and proceeds  indefinitely for the rest of the search  we implemented this model using a state  transition diagram which is a tool  commonly used in computer science for  things like autonomous driving w